	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<false/>
		<!-- L-03 / M-07: NSAllowsLocalNetworking is deliberately absent here, and
		     absent means false. Setting it true permits cleartext HTTP to every
		     .local name, unqualified hostname and RFC 1918 address — a far broader
		     exemption than a financial app with two pinned HTTPS hosts has any use
		     for, and one that would let a future feature (or a dependency) consume
		     plaintext from an attacker-controlled box on the user's Wi-Fi. Its
		     absence keeps this policy consistent with the Android
		     cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" base config.

		     React Native does need it in development: a debug build on a physical
		     device fetches its bundle and opens its HMR websocket over
		     http://<mac-lan-ip>:8081. An Info.plist holds one value per key across
		     all configurations, so that exemption cannot live in this file without
		     also shipping. It is instead injected into the *built* Info.plist
		     inside the .app by ios/scripts/ats-local-networking.sh, which runs as
		     the "[M-07] ATS local networking (Debug only)" build phase and adds the
		     key only when CONFIGURATION=Debug. For every other configuration the
		     same script strips the key and fails the build if any cleartext
		     exemption survives in the built product.

		     Do not add NSAllowsLocalNetworking, or an NSExceptionDomains entry with
		     NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads, to this file. Two build phases
		     reject it: the one above, and "[H-07] Release security guards", which
		     re-reads both this file and the built product before a release build
		     completes. A genuine local-network feature would need its own narrowly
		     scoped NSExceptionDomains entry plus a written reason here — not this
		     key, which opens the whole local network. -->
		<!-- H-03: certificate pinning. Mirrors the Android pin-set in
		     android/app/src/main/res/xml/network_security_config.xml — the two
		     MUST be updated together or the platforms diverge silently.

		     NSPinnedCAIdentities pins CA certificates in the chain (not the leaf).
		     Primary is the GeoTrust TLS RSA CA G1 intermediate that issues both
		     hosts; backup is DigiCert Global Root G2 so a reissue from a different
		     DigiCert intermediate still validates.

		     H-02 — WHAT IS NOT PINNED, AND WHY: security/pinning-policy.json, one
		     entry per host with a reason and a review date. It is enforced, not
		     documentation: the Android release build fails if a host in
		     environments/*.js is neither pinned nor exempt, and fails if this file
		     and the Android pin-set name different hosts. That parity check exists
		     because coverage on iOS matters more, not less — ATS still honours
		     user-installed certificates, so an unpinned host here can be intercepted
		     with a configuration profile alone, where Android's <base-config> trusts
		     system anchors only.

		     WARNING — unlike Android's <pin-set expiration>, ATS has no expiry and
		     no fail-open. If these pins stop matching, the iOS app cannot reach the
		     API at all and the only remedy is an App Store release.

		     M-03 — the expiry date is deliberately NOT repeated here. It lives in
		     security/pinning-policy.json (pinSet.iosExpiration), which the release
		     build phase reads to warn ahead of the date and which App.tsx reads to
		     report the fleet's pin state to Sentry. A date in a comment is the one
		     copy nothing can check, so it is the copy that goes stale silently while
		     still looking authoritative. Re-verify the chain with:
		       ./scripts/compute-spki-pins.sh

		     LAST VERIFIED: 2026-08-05. Both hosts chain through GeoTrust TLS RSA
		     CA G1 (primary pin) under DigiCert Global Root G2 (backup pin); both
		     SPKI hashes below match the live chain exactly. -->
		<key>NSPinnedDomains</key>
		<dict>
			<key>api.exchangapay.com</key>
			<dict>
				<key>NSIncludesSubdomains</key>
				<false/>
				<key>NSPinnedCAIdentities</key>
				<array>
					<dict>
						<key>SPKI-SHA256-BASE64</key>
						<string>SDG5orEv8iX6MNenIAxa8nQFNpROB/6+llsZdXHZNqs=</string>
					</dict>
					<dict>
						<key>SPKI-SHA256-BASE64</key>
						<string>i7WTqTvh0OioIruIfFR4kMPnBqrS2rdiVPl/s2uC/CY=</string>
					</dict>
				</array>
			</dict>
			<key>tstapi.exchangapay.com</key>
			<dict>
				<key>NSIncludesSubdomains</key>
				<false/>
				<key>NSPinnedCAIdentities</key>
				<array>
					<dict>
						<key>SPKI-SHA256-BASE64</key>
						<string>SDG5orEv8iX6MNenIAxa8nQFNpROB/6+llsZdXHZNqs=</string>
					</dict>
					<dict>
						<key>SPKI-SHA256-BASE64</key>
						<string>i7WTqTvh0OioIruIfFR4kMPnBqrS2rdiVPl/s2uC/CY=</string>
					</dict>
				</array>
			</dict>
		</dict>
	</dict>
